 ### [Polynomial and Spline Kernels](9995-polynomial-and-spline-kernels.md)
-This family defines splatting kernels using piecewise polynomial functions and spline basis functions to approximate volumetric density. It addresses the need for kernels with compact support and specific smoothness properties (continuity of derivatives) that differ from the standard Gaussian or EWA splats. It sets itself apart from [EWA Splatting](1069-ewa-splatting.md) by focusing on the algebraic form of the radial basis function rather than just the covariance-based footprint approximation, and from [Splat Extent and Projection](1792-splat-extent-and-projection.md) by defining the internal weight decay function.
+Polynomial and Spline Kernels replace the standard Gaussian reconstruction kernel in splatting with piecewise polynomial or spline functions to improve rendering accuracy and visual fidelity. They solve the problem of visual artifacts and blurring inherent in Gaussian kernels by providing kernels with compact support, specific decay properties, or tunable shapes that better preserve high-frequency features like edges and small textures. They set themselves apart from siblings like [EWA Splatting](1069-ewa-splatting.md) by focusing on the mathematical form of the kernel function itself rather than just the projection geometry, and from [Splat Extent and Projection](1792-splat-extent-and-projection.md) by defining the internal weight distribution within the footprint.
+
+### [Texture Stretch Weighting](11508-texture-stretch-weighting.md)
+Texture Stretch Weighting (wTS) is a weighting mechanism used in point-based neural rendering to mitigate artifacts caused by weighted average pooling when handling view-dependent effects. It compensates for the distortion of splats in novel views by penalizing pixels at grazing angles, thereby increasing the influence of front-facing views. Unlike standard isotropic weighting, it accounts for the anisotropic stretching of the splat footprint due to perspective projection.
 
